fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1096", description: some(value: "A LIST LITERAL in a rule-body goal argument gives a WRONG ANSWER: 'List.member(9, [7])' SUCCEEDS.\n\nMEASURED, with a controlled comparison -- the two fixtures differ ONLY in how the list is written:\n\n  namespace up4\n    import anthill.prelude.{List, Int64, Bool}\n    import anthill.prelude.List.{cons, nil}\n    rule yes(?m) :- List.member(7, cons(head: 7, tail: nil)), ?m = 1\n    rule no(?m)  :- List.member(9, cons(head: 7, tail: nil)), ?m = 1\n  end\n  -- up4.yes => 1 solution   CORRECT\n  -- up4.no  => 0 solutions  CORRECT\n\n  namespace up5\n    import anthill.prelude.{List, Int64, Bool}\n    rule yes(?m) :- List.member(7, [7]), ?m = 1\n    rule no(?m)  :- List.member(9, [7]), ?m = 1\n  end\n  -- up5.yes => 1 solution   CORRECT\n  -- up5.no  => 1 solution   *** WRONG -- 9 is not a member of [7] ***\n\nSo the goal answers TRUE for a membership test that is FALSE, silently, with no diagnostic. It is not an argument-order or spelling artifact: the cons-spelled twin is correct, and the ONLY difference is the '[7]' literal.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN A TYPICAL WRONG ANSWER. It is in the prelude's most-used predicate, in the most natural spelling a user would reach for, and it is reachable from ANY rule body. A membership test that answers true for everything makes every rule whose body filters on it unsound -- and the failure is INVISIBLE (the rule just has more solutions than it should, which reads as data, not as an error). Negation makes it worse: 'not member(?x, [..])' then fails where it should succeed.\n\nMEASURED CONTEXT, so the investigation does not re-derive it:\n - THE BODY IS FINE. Through eval, 'List.member(7, [7])' => Bool(true) and 'List.member(9, [7])' => Bool(false) -- BOTH CORRECT. So the literal desugars correctly in an OPERATION BODY and the defect is specific to the rule-body GOAL path.\n - THE DERIVED RELATIONAL VIEW IS FINE. 'List.member' is a rule-less bodied Bool op, so a bare goal routes through 'bare_bodied_bool_relation' to 'eq(member(args), true)' (WI-580 / design 3.3). With cons-spelled arguments that route answers correctly (1/0 above), so the routing is not the fault either.\n - CONCLUSION FROM THOSE TWO: the fault is in what the '[7]' ARGUMENT becomes on the rule-body path, not in member, not in the routing, and not in eval.\n\nHYPOTHESIS, NOT MEASURED -- do not take it as the diagnosis. 'ListLiteral -> cons/nil' desugaring is CONTEXT-KEYED: WI-007 replaced an unconditional rewrite with one driven by the surrounding entity field type ('convert_term_with_expected' / 'find_list_element_type' in kb/load.rs), and WI-936 records a load-ORDER dependency in the same area (field types must be known before conversion). A rule-body goal argument may supply no expected type, leaving a raw ListLiteral term that then unifies with the goal's variable structurally -- which would explain a spurious success. VERIFY THIS; the shape of the surviving term is the first thing to look at.\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n - 'List.member(9, [7])' as a rule-body goal answers ZERO solutions, driven by a test that FAILS before the fix -- not a load-clean assertion;\n - the CONTROL kept and stated at the site: the cons-spelled twin (correct today) must still answer 1/0, so the fix is attributable;\n - the eval-path twin also kept: 'List.member(9, [7])' in an operation body must still evaluate to Bool(false);\n - a NEGATION row -- 'not member(?x, [..])' -- since that is where a spurious success flips a rule's meaning the other way;\n - the fix is at the ONE place a list literal is lowered, not a special case in the goal path: if rule-body goal arguments are a context the desugaring never reached, find every OTHER argument position with the same gap (a fact head, a constraint, a proof obligation, an operation call in a rule body) and say which were measured. 'Many producers, one rule';\n - a literal of length > 1 and an EMPTY literal '[]' both driven, since a one-element literal may be a special case;\n - say at the test site which tests fail when the change is backed out and which pass either way by design;\n - full workspace green via scripts/test.sh (a loader/lowering change => FULL workspace).\n\nSOURCE: found while measuring how List.member is meant to be called (a design discussion about relations vs boolean predicates, 052 Open-7). Not related to that design question -- it is a pre-existing defect the measurement happened to expose."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none), ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-14T13:53:08Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1096", author: "user", content: "SCOPE CORRECTED BEFORE ANY WORK STARTED — the population in this ticket's description is keyed on the wrong thing, and is too narrow.\n\nTHE DESCRIPTION SAYS the population is 'an operation with -> Bool, NO body, and relational clauses' — exactly two, Set.member and Set.subset. THAT KEYS THE CONVENTION ON HOW A PREDICATE IS DEFINED, which is wrong: the naming is about what the operation IS (a boolean face), not about the form its definition happens to take. List.member is the SAME predicate written with a body and zero clauses; measured, it is equally not citable as a Relation. A rule keyed on body-existence would rename an operation when its definition was rewritten — from clauses to a body or back — which is not a naming rule at all.\n\nTHE KEY IS THE Bool RETURN TYPE. 052 Open-7 has been corrected to say so (and relation.anthill's header with it).\n\nRE-MEASURED POPULATION, over stdlib, anthill-stl, examples, anthill-todo and anthill-cpp-gen: 59 Bool-returning operations, 7 already is-prefixed. Three classes are outside the convention, each for a stated reason rather than by count:\n  - SPEC-OWNED, 27 — eq, neq, gt/gte/lt/lte, less, compare, and every carrier implementing them. The spec owns the name; a provider implements what it provides. This is the Set.eq exception the description already carries, and it is by far the largest exclusion.\n  - EFFECTFUL, 4+ — Store.flush, Store.retract, Emitter.send, MutableCollection.insert. A Bool here is a success/status result, not a proposition. Note this is not an invented rule: the resolver ALREADY gates bare_bodied_bool_relation effect-free, on the stated ground that 'an effectful body is not a logical relation' (design/abstract-interpreter-and-rules.md).\n  - CONNECTIVES ON Bool, 4 — and / or / not. Operations ON truth values, not propositions ABOUT a subject.\n\nThat leaves ~14 genuine predicates, SPANNING BOTH DEFINITION FORMS, which is the point:\n  Set.member, Set.subset (clauses); List.member (body); Map.contains; String.contains / startsWith / endsWith; reflect.ground / nonvar / can_be_sort; platform.exists / has_property; parse.has_binding; Relation.where.\n\nTHE COUNT IS APPROXIMATE AT ITS EDGES AND THE TICKET MUST SETTLE IT, rather than inheriting my number: kernel.cut and kernel.push_choice landed in the predicate bucket and are resolver CONTROL primitives, not predicates; and at least one effectful op (MutableCollection.insert) escaped my census's effect test, which looked ahead only three lines for an 'effects' clause. So the first task is an exact list, by a test-driven census rather than a regex — the same instrument-first discipline the rest of this ticket's acceptance asks for.\n\nWHAT THIS CHANGES ABOUT THE WORK: the migration is ~14 renames rather than 2, and it now spans both spellings — so the acceptance rows must include a BODIED predicate (List.member) as well as a clause-defined one (Set.member). Everything else in the description stands, including the three things that break under a textual rename ([simp] laws, the dispatch surface, and Set.member being the canonical worked example in six compiler doc comments).\n\nSTILL TRUE AND WORTH RE-STATING: zero .anthill consumers of Set.member / Set.subset outside set.anthill. List.member's consumers were NOT measured and must be before it is renamed.\n\nSOURCE: user correction — 'the is-convention should not depend from body existence'.", at: "2026-08-14T09:09:52Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1096", author: "user", content: "A HAZARD THIS TICKET MUST ANSWER BEFORE RENAMING List.member, found while checking whether WI-939 item 4's refusal interacts with this migration. It does not — measured, item 4 has zero corpus hits and the stdlib loads clean under it — but the QUESTION it raised is real and this ticket owns it.\n\nWHERE DOES List.member's RELATION FACE COME FROM? Under the convention the plain name is the relation and is<Name> the boolean. For Set.member that is nearly mechanical: the clauses ARE the relation, so they keep the plain name (as a rule, minting a Goal and becoming citable) and isMember becomes the boolean over Relation[Unit] non-emptiness. For List.member there are NO clauses to keep — it is a body, and WI-580 RETIRED its two ':-' twins DELIBERATELY, on a soundness ground stated at list.anthill:45-49: they branched on cons(head: ?x, ...), structural UNIFICATION, 'diverging unsoundly from the body's eq(head, x) (the declared Eq) for a type whose eq is not structural equality'. That was called the headline soundness fix.\n\nSO THE OBVIOUS MOVE IS FORBIDDEN. Hand-writing clauses for List.member to give it a citable relation face would REINTRODUCE exactly the unsoundness WI-580 removed. This ticket must not do that, and must say at the site that it did not.\n\nTHE THREE OPTIONS, none of them free, and this ticket must pick one and RECORD the reason:\n (a) List keeps only the boolean face — rename to isMember and DO NOT provide a relation named member. Honest, but then the convention's plain name is absent for List while present for Set, and a reader cannot tell from the naming which types have a relation face.\n (b) Derive the relation from the body. bare_bodied_bool_relation already routes a bare Bool goal to eq(op(args), true) using the declared Eq by construction — the sound path. What is missing is that this derived reading is NOT citable as a Relation VALUE (cites_a_relation needs a Goal/Rule kind, and a bodied operation mints neither — measured). Making it citable is a typer change, is bigger than a rename, and is arguably 052's own missing arm rather than this ticket's.\n (c) Defer List.member entirely — migrate only the clause-defined predicates now and file the bodied ones separately.\n\nRECOMMENDATION: (c) for this ticket unless (b) turns out small, with (a) explicitly rejected — an inconsistent convention is worse than a staged one.\n\nNOTE THE ASYMMETRY THIS EXPOSES, which is worth stating in 052 whichever option wins: a CLAUSE-defined predicate can carry both faces (clauses are citable), while a BODY-defined one can carry only the boolean today. That is a real limitation of the two-faces story and not a naming problem — and it is the same one-way gap 052 Open-7 already records ('the derivation runs body -> relation only'). If (b) lands, the convention becomes uniform; until then it is not, and the docs should not imply otherwise.", at: "2026-08-14T09:57:40Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1096", author: "user", content: "OPTION (c) IS WITHDRAWN, AND MY RECOMMENDATION IN THE PREVIOUS FEEDBACK WAS BACKWARDS. User objection, and it is decisive: 'a rule should hold or not'. A convention applied to part of its population is not a convention.\n\nTHE ERROR WAS CONFLATING TWO THINGS. The rule is A Bool-VALUED PREDICATE IS NAMED is<Name>. That is a statement about NAMING and it can hold uniformly across the whole population TODAY, both definition forms, whatever the relation face situation. Whether the PLAIN name then gets FILLED by a relation is a SEPARATE fact, answered per type by whether a relation face is citable there.\n\nRead that way the options sort out the other way round from my last note:\n\n (a) RENAME ALL OF THEM — TAKE THIS. Every Bool predicate in the population becomes is<Name>. The rule holds everywhere, immediately. Set additionally gets a relation at the plain name 'member' because its clauses are citable; List simply has no 'member' yet, and that ABSENCE violates nothing — no type is left with a plain-named boolean, which is the only thing the rule forbids. My previous note rejected (a) on the ground that 'a reader cannot tell from the naming which types have a relation face'. That objection is wrong: the naming was never meant to advertise which faces exist, only to stop the boolean from occupying the relation's name.\n\n (c) WITHDRAWN — rename only the clause-defined predicates. This leaves List.member a PLAIN-NAMED BOOLEAN beside Set.isMember: the rule violated for half the population and for the SAME CONCEPT (membership), which is precisely the incoherence I rejected (a) for. I recommended it anyway. It is off the table.\n\n (b) IS NO LONGER AN ALTERNATIVE — it is an INDEPENDENT FOLLOW-UP, and does not gate this ticket. Making a body-derived relation citable as a Relation VALUE (today cites_a_relation needs a Goal/Rule kind and a bodied operation mints neither, so List.member is reachable as a GOAL but not as a VALUE) is what later FILLS the plain name for body-defined predicates. Nothing about the rename waits on it, and the rename does not make it harder: it frees the plain name, which is exactly what (b) would want to occupy.\n\nSO THIS TICKET'S SCOPE IS THE WHOLE ~14, not the 2 the description names and not the clause-defined subset. Both spellings migrate together.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE ACCEPTANCE, and it is a REAL COST that was hidden while (c) was on the table: List.member's CONSUMERS were never measured. Set.member's were (zero .anthill outside set.anthill), which is what made the 2-site version look cheap. List.member is a prelude operation with an op-scoped requires Eq[T] (WI-562/WI-448) and is far more likely to have callers -- in stdlib bodies, in examples, in tests, and in cpp-gen/scaland which pin stdlib shapes. MEASURE THAT FIRST; it may well dominate the ticket, and if it does, that is an argument about HOW to stage the rename (e.g. a deprecation window), never about applying the rule to only some types.\n\nSOURCE: user objection to the previous feedback's option (c).", at: "2026-08-14T10:03:34Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1096", author: "user", content: "SPELLING UPDATE ONLY, the defect is unchanged. `List.member` was renamed to `List.contains` with the arguments swapped to CONTAINER FIRST (commit e7d9390d), so this ticket's repro is now written:\n\n  rule yes(?m) :- List.contains([7], 7), ?m = 1   -- 1 solution   CORRECT\n  rule no(?m)  :- List.contains([7], 9), ?m = 1   -- 1 solution   *** STILL WRONG ***\n\nand the correct-answering control is `List.contains(cons(head: 7, tail: nil), 9)` => 0. Everything else in the description holds as written: the body is fine through eval, the derived relational view is fine with cons-spelled arguments, and the fault is in what the literal ARGUMENT becomes on the rule-body goal path.\n\nNOTE the new test `wi939_contains_rename_test::list_contains_as_a_rule_body_goal` deliberately uses the CONS spelling for exactly this reason -- so it pins the rename without tripping on this bug. When this ticket is fixed, that test is the natural place to add the literal-spelled row.", at: "2026-08-14T11:41:13Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1096", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED. The hypothesis in the description was RIGHT, and the fix is the opposite of what \"make the hint reach the goal path\" would have been.\n\nWHAT THE MEASUREMENT SHOWED. The literal survives un-desugared into the rule body — confirmed by dumping the stored clause: `no(?#0) :- contains([7], 9), eq(?#0, 1)` for the literal against `no(?#0) :- contains(cons(head: 7, tail: nil), 9), eq(?#0, 1)` for the twin. So `contains` cannot destructure it, the derived relational reading never discharges, and the goal rides out as an UNDISCHARGED RESIDUAL. The ticket's \"answers TRUE\" is precisely that: the goal is never DECIDED, and a conditional solution still counts as a solution to whatever asked. Worth separating, because it means `up5.yes` was not right either — it answered 1 for the wrong reason.\n\nTHE FIX, ONE PLACE. The site is the `ListLiteral` branch of `Loader::convert_term_with_expected` (kb/load.rs). Before: lower only when a DECLARED type is List-shaped. After: lower unless a declared type NAMES another collection. No goal-path special case and no second lowering site.\n\nTHE ALTERNATIVE, REFUTED BY MEASUREMENT. Supplying the expected type for operation-call arguments — WI-936's move one channel over — was considered and rejected:\n - it does not reach the other positions at all. A rule head, a plain relation's fact head and a bare `?xs = [1, 2]` declare no argument types and never will. All three measured broken (0 solutions where the cons-spelled twin answered 1);\n - it would have made one of them WORSE. `eq`'s parameter type is the type variable `T`, so supplying it turns `expected = None` (lower) into `expected = Some(T)` (decline) — the fix would have broken `?xs = [1, 2]` while claiming to fix the goal path;\n - and it costs an operation-signature pre-pass to be load-order-independent, which is a rewrite of `load_operation`'s signature lowering.\n\n`expected == None` was ONE NAME FOR TWO QUESTIONS — \"this position declares a non-List collection\" and \"nobody declares anything here\" — and the branch answered the first for both. Only the DEFAULT can answer the second.\n\nEVERY OTHER ARGUMENT POSITION, MEASURED (the \"many producers, one rule\" row), each beside its own cons-spelled twin:\n\n  position                                       literal  twin\n  op-call argument as a GOAL                     1 cond.   0    BROKEN -> fixed\n  op-call argument for its VALUE (length)        1 cond.   1    BROKEN -> fixed\n  rule head (rule pass([1, 2, 3]))                  0      1    BROKEN -> fixed\n  plain relation's fact head                        0      1    BROKEN -> fixed\n  bare ?xs = [1, 2], then ?xs = cons(...)        1 cond.  1 cond.  see below\n  entity field declared List[T = Int64]             1      1    already correct (WI-007/936)\n  entity field declared Set[T = Int64]              0      0    DECLINES, by design\n  bounded quantifier slot (forall ?x in [...])      1/0    1/0  already correct\n\nRow 5 does not discriminate on the answer: two delayed `eq`s in a row suspend on the unbound `?xs` (WI-519) before the spine shape matters. It was measured on the stored clause instead — `eq(?_, [1, 2])` before, `eq(?_, cons(head: 1, tail: cons(head: 2, tail: nil)))` after — and is driven in the grounded form (through a rule head) in the test.\n\nThe bounded quantifier is the interesting negative: it was ALREADY correct, because its reader `bounded_list_elements` is carrier-neutral and carries an explicit `ListLiteral` arm beside its `cons` one. Its row passes with the change backed out, which is the measurement. That arm stays — a `Set`-declared slot and every reflect-built `ListLiteral` still reach it — and its doc comment, which justified the arm by \"a bounded-quant collection slot carries no List-typed context\", was corrected: that sentence stopped being true here and would have read as licence to delete the arm.\n\nTWO POSITIONS WERE READ, NOT DRIVEN, each with its reason. A CONSTRAINT body: the kernel has no runtime constraint checker (spec §1 states the execution boundary is narrower than the logical reading; `anthill check` is a scaffold), so there is nothing to drive — the body converts through the same function. A PROOF obligation: same function, nothing about a proof reaches the literal branch differently.\n\nWHAT /code-review FOUND, AND IT FOUND THE SAME DEFECT AGAIN. The first version keyed the declining branch on \"not List-shaped\", which is STILL one name for two questions — it declines both for a type that NAMES another collection and for one that names nothing in particular. Three real findings, all verified by measurement before fixing and all fixed here:\n\n 1. HIGH — A TYPE-PARAMETER-TYPED FIELD RESURRECTED THE BUG ONE FIELD DEEP. `entity Box(v: T)` and, more importantly, `Option.some(value: T)` — which every bare `some([...])` passes through. Measured with the first fix already in: `boxed(Box(v: ?l)), contains(?l, 9)` answered 1 conditional with `residual: eq(contains([1, 2, 3], 9), true)`, byte-for-byte the original defect; `some(cons(head: 1, tail: ?))` answered 0 where the identical un-wrapped literal answered 1. A `T` says the position is GENERIC, which is what an absent declaration says; it is not a rival collection. Fixed with a new `typing::is_type_variable` (TypeVar / FlexVar / Skolem), stated beside `is_option_type` for the same reason it exists.\n\n 2. MEDIUM — THE DEFAULT SILENTLY DROPPED NAMED ARGS. `[...]` parses to positional children only (WI-560 removed the tail form), so a `ListLiteral` carrying named args was written BY NAME as the reflect entity — a spelling this repo uses (docs/proposals/typing_pass_spec.anthill:542). The branch reads `pos_args` alone, so `fact named(ListLiteral(elements: 1))` loaded as `nil` and `named(nil())` matched it. This was a regression the default change introduced on its own. Fixed with a `named_args.is_empty()` guard — the same test, for the same reason, that the term printer already applies.\n\n 3. LOW/MEDIUM — A COLLECTION OF LISTS LOWERED ANYWAY. `find_list_element_type` descended into ANY parameterized type's bindings, so a `Set[T = List[T = Int64]]`-declared field lowered its `[1, 2]` to a cons spine (measured: the cons pattern matched) and was then NOT wrapped, because the `some(...)` half correctly saw a non-Option. The behaviour predates this ticket, but the ticket makes \"a declared non-List collection keeps its literal as written\" NORMATIVE, so it became a spec violation. Fixed by gating the descent on `is_option_type` — the descent and the WI-408 wrap are two halves of ONE decision and now key on the same predicate. Corpus-checked first: every wrapper-of-List in stdlib/examples/anthill-todo is an `Option`, and the `Option[T = List[T = String]]` path is kept green by its own control row.\n\nTwo more findings were stale CLAIMS rather than behaviour, and both were real:\n 4. My comment asserted a `Value::Node` (value-in-type) declared field type would still decline. It would not — the `exp` read narrows to a ground TermId, so it arrives as `None` and takes the List default. Latent (no such collection sort exists), and the comment now says so instead of the opposite.\n 5. My re-keyed WI-936 control claimed to catch \"the declaration arrived late\" — but its entity and fact were both in the same fixture with the entity FIRST, so a load-order back-out would not have shown. Fixed by FORWARD-DECLARING the entity (fact before `entity Bagged`), which puts the row under the same dependency as that file's measured subject rows, rather than by softening the claim.\n\nAnd one doc: `reflect.anthill`'s `entity ListLiteral` comment still said the typing process rewrites it from the expected type, with no mention that the loader now lowers it by default.\n\nTESTS. New `wi1096_list_literal_lowering_test`, 14 rows in `wi_tests`. Backed out, MEASURED rather than asserted: exactly 6 fail, 8 pass. The six are the goal row, the negation row, the sizes row (length > 1 and the empty `[]`, which is now `nil()` and not a zero-arg `ListLiteral()`), the head/fact row, the op-call-value row, and the type-parameter row. The eight controls are the cons twin, the eval path (an operation body's `[...]` is an `Expr::ListLit` OCCURRENCE — a different carrier, and a fix that \"worked\" by changing eval would have been fixing the wrong one), the bounded quantifier, the declared-List field, the Option-wrapped field, the declared-Set field, the collection-of-lists field, and the named-arg row.\n\nAlso added `wi939_contains_rename_test::list_contains_over_a_literal_as_a_rule_body_goal` — the row the feedback asked for, beside the cons-spelled one that was written to avoid this defect.\n\nONE PRE-EXISTING TEST MOVED, AND IT IS THE INTERESTING ONE. `wi936_field_type_load_order_test::an_untyped_list_literal_stays_flat_in_either_order` was WI-936's own CONTROL, whose stated job was \"to stop the fix from being desugar everywhere\" — it pinned exactly the behaviour this ticket removes. It is re-keyed onto the case WI-007 genuinely protects: the subject is now a forward-declared `Set[T = Int64]` field, and the undeclared literal ANSWERS. The re-keyed control is STRICTLY SHARPER: it fails both on \"desugars unconditionally\" and on \"the declaration arrived late\", where the old one detected only the first. WI-936's own back-out count of six is left as measured and flagged as now a lower bound, not re-measured.\n\nWhole-workspace blast radius of the code change: that ONE test, across all 29 binaries.\n\nKNOWN, NOT CLAIMED: in a RULE BODY a named-arg `ListLiteral` still loses its named arg further downstream, at the term->occurrence build (`Expr::ListLit` is a flat vector by WI-560's decision). Pre-existing, unchanged by this ticket, and the new test asserts only the fact side, which is what this change reached.\n\nDOCS. kernel-language.md §4.6 said \"in any other position the literal is left as written\" — that sentence was the defect, written down. Replaced with the rule and its three non-gap cases (undeclared, type-parameter-typed, Option-payload), the collection-of-lists clarification, the named-arg exclusion, and the §4.3 table row. Proposal 019 keeps its text and gets a short update note pointing at the spec.\n\nGREEN. Full workspace via scripts/test.sh: RUST_EXIT=0, 29 binaries. scaland `sbt test`: 490 passed, 0 failed — scaland has no ListLiteral desugaring at all (measured: the Rust WI-007 rewrite was never ported), so it needed no change and confirms none was made by accident.\n\nNOT WIDENED, one observation left as an observation: an undischarged residual counts in `solutions.len()`. That is the designed conditional-answer semantics (the CLI prints \"1 conditional (residual goals undischarged)\" and the spec says such a goal is never silently decided), not a defect — but it is what turned a NON-DECISION into something that reads as a wrong answer, and it is why the tests here count DEFINITE solutions rather than solutions.", at: "2026-08-14T13:53:06Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1096", author: "claude", content: "FOLLOW-UP TO THE DELIVERY, after a SECOND `/code-review` pass run against the delivered commit (8f188953) rather than against the working tree. The first pass reviewed the fix; it did not review the fixes the fix needed. This is what the second pass found.\n\nA LIVE REGRESSION I SHIPPED, and it is the ticket's own acceptance clause turned back on me. The acceptance said \"find every OTHER argument position with the same gap\". I enumerated six and MISSED A SEVENTH: `convert_query_term` -- the QUERY converter, which `wi366_value_in_type_facts_test` already names \"a FOURTH term-position\". It has no ListLiteral handling at all. So after my change the loader lowered an undeclared literal while a query pattern still built the flat one, and the two stopped matching.\n\nMEASURED at 8f188953, on `fact stored([1, 2, 3])` with no declared type for `stored`:\n\n  anthill query 'stored(?x)'          =>  ?x = [1, 2, 3]     1 solution\n  anthill query 'stored([1, 2, 3])'   =>  NO SOLUTIONS\n\nThe user copies the answer the tool just printed back into a query and gets nothing. Invisible for the same reason the original defect was: `TermPrinter` renders a flat `ListLiteral` and a ground `cons` spine IDENTICALLY as `[1, 2, 3]`, so both sides LOOK the same at every point a human inspects them.\n\nAND THE DESYNC WAS OLDER THAN MY CHANGE. Measured at the same commit: `held(Holder(xs: [1, 2]))`, where `xs` IS declared `List[T = Int64]`, also answered ZERO -- unmatchable by a literal query since WI-007 gave the loader its rewrite and gave the query converter nothing. My change did not create that half; it widened the desync from \"declared List fields\" to \"every undeclared position\", which is the majority of patterns anyone writes.\n\nTHE FIX IS THE ONE THE TICKET ASKED FOR IN THE FIRST PLACE -- one place, not two. The decision is now a shared `list_literal_lowering(kb, functor, has_named_args, expected)` that BOTH converters call, and `convert_query_term` gained the `expected` hint (read from `entity_field_types` exactly as the loader reads it, via a shared `declared_field_type`). The two cannot disagree about what `[...]` is because there is one function that answers it. That also closes the pre-existing WI-007 half for free: all four rows now agree.\n\nDRIVEN, with the controls that make it attributable: the literal a query prints matches (was 0); a DIFFERENT literal does NOT (or \"matches anything\" would pass); the declared-`List` field matches (the WI-007 half); the declared-`Set` field still matches, because there BOTH sides decline together. Pinned by its own back-out: drop the `list_literal_lowering` call in `convert_query_term` and exactly that one row fails (measured, 14 passed / 1 failed).\n\nTWO STALE CLAIMS, both mine, both fixed:\n - `convert_term_with_expected`'s own docstring still read \"when `expected` is a `List`-shaped type, `ListLiteral` is rewritten to `cons/nil`; otherwise it stays in the KB as `ListLiteral`\" -- the pre-WI-1096 default, three lines above the branch that now does the reverse, and the very sentence kernel-language.md 4.6 calls \"the defect, written down\". A reader auditing from the signature got the wrong rule.\n - my new `resolve.rs` note claimed \"every reflect-built `ListLiteral` term reaches here unchanged\". True only IN MEMORY: printed to disk it renders as `[...]`, so a reload puts it back through the loader and it returns a cons spine. Narrowed to say exactly that.\n\nONE FINDING FILED RATHER THAN FIXED -- WI-1099. A POSITIONAL `ListLiteral(a, b)` written BY NAME is byte-identical to `[a, b]` after parse, so it now lowers too: the reflect entity is unwritable in a term position, and `occurrence_to_term`'s positional term twin is unmatchable by a source-written pattern. Zero corpus users (grep found only the named-arg `case ListLiteral(elements: elems)` in an operation body, which is expression world), so it is latent, not a live break. The fix has a precedent and is small -- a parse-level surface marker on the WI-927 `mark_type_application` model -- but WHETHER a written `ListLiteral(a, b)` should mean the reflect entity or just spell a list is a surface-semantics decision that WI-927 answered for its own case and this one must answer explicitly. That is why it is a ticket and not a line, and the gap is cited at the site (`list_literal_lowering`'s doc) with the ticket number, per \"a gap with a reason has no owner\".\n\nWHAT I TAKE FROM IT. The first review caught me rebuilding the conflation one level in; the second caught me applying \"many producers, one rule\" to six producers and stopping. Both are the same failure of the same acceptance clause. The durable fix is not the enumeration -- it is that the two converters now share ONE decision function, so the seventh producer could not have been missed.\n\nGREEN after the change: full workspace via scripts/test.sh (29 binaries), scaland `sbt test` unchanged.", at: "2026-08-14T14:50:26Z")
